    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Maurice de Sully had the earlier cathedral demolished and began a much larger Gothic church in 1163; four phases of building under two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> brought it largely to completion by 1260.</li><li>The building was stripped and damaged in the French Revolution, fell into disrepair, and was restored between 1844 and 1864 by Viollet-le-Duc after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>'s novel of 1831 drew attention to its state.</li><li>A fire on 15 April 2019 destroyed the flèche and the oak roof beams; the main structure survived, and the cathedral reopened in December 2024.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2023 archaeologists found thousands of iron staples in the cathedral, some dating from the early 1160s, and concluded that Notre-Dame is the first known Gothic cathedral where iron was used massively to bind stones.</p></aside>
    </section>

