    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1163</span><span class="rf-text">Construction began with the laying of the cornerstone in the presence of King Louis VII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1225</span><span class="rf-text">The west rose window, the first and smallest of the three roses, was made</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1431</span><span class="rf-text">The ten-year-old Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the cathedral</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the cathedral was rededicated to the Cult of Reason, and its treasures were destroyed or plundered</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1804</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crowned himself Emperor of the French in the cathedral on 2 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text">Jean-Baptiste Lassus and Eugène Viollet-le-Duc were appointed to a restoration that lasted until 1864</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2019</span><span class="rf-text">A fire destroyed the flèche and the roof <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2024</span><span class="rf-text">The restored cathedral reopened in a ceremony on 7 December</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii.jpg" alt="Pope Alexander III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Becket_bids_farewell_to_the_Pope_-_Becket_Leaves_(c.1220-1240),_f._1v_-_BL_Loan_MS_88_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Possibly Matthew Paris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>It is believed that before the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> a Gallo-Roman temple dedicated to Jupiter stood on the site of Notre-Dame, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>. Evidence for this includes the Pillar of the Boatmen, discovered beneath the cathedral. In the 4th or 5th century a large early Christian church, the Cathedral of Saint Étienne, was built there close to the royal palace. It was roughly half the size of the later Notre-Dame, 70 metres long, and was divided into nave and four aisles by marble columns and decorated with mosaics. The last church before the cathedral was a Romanesque remodelling of Saint-Étienne, which was found to be unfit for the growing population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>In 1160 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Paris, Maurice de Sully, decided to build a new and much larger church. He summarily demolished the earlier cathedral and recycled its materials. He decided that the new church should be built in the Gothic style, which had been inaugurated at the royal abbey of Saint Denis in the late 1130s. The chronicler Jean de Saint-Victor recorded that construction began between 24 March and 25 April 1163 with the laying of the cornerstone in the presence of King Louis VII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus.jpg" alt="Mary, mother of Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Mary, mother of Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madonna_Advocata.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Asia, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The cathedral is dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, "Our Lady", and is considered one of the finest examples of French Gothic architecture. Several attributes set it apart from the earlier Romanesque style: its pioneering use of the rib vault and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flying-buttress">flying buttress</a>, its enormous and colourful rose windows, and the naturalism and abundance of its sculptural decoration. The two towers are 69 metres high, and the west rose window is 9.6 metres across. The transept roses are larger and contain a greater proportion of glass, because the new system of buttresses made the nave walls thinner and stronger.</p><p>The Gothic cathedral was a liber pauperum, a "poor people's book", covered with sculpture that illustrated biblical stories for the many parishioners who could not read. The tympanum of the central west portal shows the Last Judgment, the right portal the coronation of the Virgin and the left portal the lives of saints important to Parisians, particularly Saint Anne. All the sculpture on the façades was originally painted and gilded. Grotesques such as the chimera and the strix stood as symbols of the evil that threatened those who did not follow the church's teaching, while the gargoyles, added about 1240, were rain spouts that threw water clear of the walls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Four phases of construction took place under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> Maurice de Sully and Eudes de Sully, who were not related, according to masters whose names have been lost. The first phase built the choir and its two ambulatories; according to Robert of Torigni the choir was completed in 1177 and the high altar was consecrated on 19 May 1182. The second phase, from 1182 to 1190, built the nave to the height of the clerestories, and from 1190 the bases of the façade were put in place. Four-part rather than six-part rib vaults made the roofs stronger and allowed them to be higher. The western façade was largely built by the time of Eudes de Sully's death in 1208 and was completed around the mid-1240s, and the two towers followed between 1225 and 1250.</p><p>In the mid-13th century the transepts were remodelled in the Rayonnant style: Jean de Chelles added a gabled portal and rose window to the north transept in the late 1240s, and Pierre de Montreuil did the same on the south from 1258. Pierre de Chelles, Jean Ravy, Jean le Bouteiller and Raymond du Temple later carried the work on. The date of the first flying buttresses is not known with precision; Andrew Tallon argued from laser scans that they were part of the original design, and the first buttresses were replaced by larger ones in the 14th century. Vault stones that fell in 2019 were quarried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vexin">Vexin</a> and presumably brought up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> by boat.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Notre-Dame de Paris&#8221; cites where it covers Construction:</p><ul><li>Dany Sandron, Notre-Dame Cathedral: nine centuries of history (The Pennsylvania State University Press, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip IV of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_IV_of_France_1285-1314.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Notre-Dame was already a public stage while building continued. Patriarch Heraclius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> called for the Third Crusade from the still incomplete cathedral in 1185, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip the Fair</a> opened the first Estates General there in 1302. In his Treatise on the Praises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of 1323, John of Jandun named it one of the city's three most important buildings and wrote that it shone out "like the sun among stars". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Saint Louis</a> IX deposited there the relics of the Passion, including the crown of thorns, that he had bought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Emperor Baldwin II. On 16 December 1431 the boy-king Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in Notre-Dame, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> was under French control. During the late 12th and early 13th centuries it was the birthplace and leading centre of the Notre-Dame school of polyphony.</p><p>Tastes and use changed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> the Gothic style fell out of fashion and the pillars and walls were covered with tapestries. Rioting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> damaged statues in 1548 as idolatrous. From 1630 the goldsmiths' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> donated a large altarpiece every year, the grands mays, and seventy-six had been given when the custom ended in 1708. In 1699 a major redecoration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> style began, and in 1756 the canons judged the interior too dark and had the medieval stained glass, except the roses, replaced with plain white panes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1789 Notre-Dame was seized as public property. It was rededicated in 1793 to the Cult of Reason and in 1794 to the Cult of the Supreme Being, and many treasures were destroyed or plundered. The twenty-eight statues of biblical kings on the west façade, mistaken for statues of French kings, were beheaded; many of the heads were found in a 1977 excavation and are displayed in the Musée de Cluny. The building was used as a warehouse. The Concordat of 1801 returned it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, and the exterior was whitewashed and the interior decorated in Neoclassical style for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s coronation in 1804.</p><p>In the decades after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it fell into such disrepair that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> officials considered demolishing it. From 1844 Lassus and Viollet-le-Duc restored it, remaking or adding decoration that Viollet-le-Duc felt was in the spirit of the original style, including a taller and more ornate flèche; the work cost over 12 million francs. In May 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> piled furniture to burn the cathedral but halted when they realised the fire would also destroy the neighbouring Hôtel-Dieu hospital. The façade was cleaned in 1963, and a renovation began in 1991 to replace weathered exterior stonework.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-hugo.jpg" alt="Victor Hugo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Hugo_by_%C3%89tienne_Carjat_1876_-_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Étienne Carjat, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> wrote the novel Notre-Dame de Paris, published in English as The Hunchback of Notre-Dame, in 1831 to save the cathedral; it was an enormous success and raised awareness of its decay. Notre-Dame hosted the funerals of many presidents of the French Republic, and on 26 August 1944 a special Mass celebrated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-paris">liberation of Paris</a>. In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> gave it the status of a minor basilica. Since 1905, like the other cathedrals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, it has been owned by the French government, with the exclusive right of use granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. It is widely recognised as a symbol of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the French nation.</p><p>After the fire of 15 April 2019 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> promised that it would be restored. Heritage academics and professionals criticised a plan to build a new flèche quickly, and over 1,170 experts signed an open letter against exempting the cathedral from heritage law. A new flèche was put in place on 16 December 2023, and the cathedral reopened on 7 December 2024. Controversy continued over a plan to replace six undamaged 19th-century windows by Viollet-le-Duc with designs by Claire Tabouret; preservationists opposed it, and 140,000 people signed a petition to keep the old windows.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Notre-Dame de Paris, begun in 1163 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Maurice de Sully and largely complete by 1260, is a Gothic cathedral known for its rib vaults, flying buttresses and rose windows. It was damaged in the Revolution, restored by Viollet-le-Duc, burned in 2019 and reopened in 2024.</em></p>

